{
  "summary": "Round 10 raised six valid issues but three have straightforward resolutions and three reveal deeper design tensions worth finalizing. (1) For dependency_notes vs pipeline_log: use a dedicated `dependency_events` list field on FeatureFile (same conditional-persistence pattern as depends_on — omit when empty, default to [] on read), with entries like {ts, event: 'dep_rejected', dep_id, dep_title, note}; the planning phase in phases.py reads this field directly rather than parsing pipeline_log, and the field is cleared when a new plan is approved, preventing stale rejection context from persisting across planning cycles. (2) Manual stage moves bypassing dependency blocking is the correct default — users should always be able to override the system. Add a warning log entry in _cmd_move_feature() when moving a blocked feature into impl_stages ('Feature moved to {stage} with unresolved dependencies: {dep_titles}'), visible in history and pipeline_log, but do NOT hard-block the move. The dependency system is advisory for humans, mandatory for auto-mode — this matches the existing pattern where auto-mode enforces cooldowns and retry limits but humans can override via manual moves. (3) The broadcast computation concern is valid but the fix is simple: add a _dependency_dirty flag on PipelineServer, set it when any depends_on edit or stage transition occurs, and only rebuild the blocked-status cache when dirty. The cache is a dict[str, bool] mapping feature IDs to blocked status, rebuilt in O(n) from the feature list. Since _broadcast_state() already iterates all features to build the state message, the marginal cost of a dict lookup per feature is negligible — the optimization is avoiding the graph rebuild, not the per-feature lookup. (4) The dual-implementation maintenance trap is the most important architectural point: extract a resolve_dependencies() module-level function in state.py that takes a list of (id, stage, depends_on) tuples and returns a dict of {id: {blocked: bool, unresolved_deps: [ids]}}. Both DependencyGraph in server.py and the CLI pipeline.py standalone check call this single function. Cycle detection is a second function detect_cycle(feature_id, proposed_deps, all_deps_map) in the same module. This keeps the resolution semantics in exactly one place while allowing different callers to manage their own caching/lifecycle. (5) The TOCTOU gap in cycle detection is theoretically real but practically a non-issue for v1: concurrent depends_on edits require two users editing different features' dependencies within the same filesystem write window (milliseconds), on a system designed for small teams. The auto-run backstop suggestion from round 10 is the right pragmatic answer — add an assert-no-cycles check at the top of the auto-run impl_stages pass that logs an error and skips the entire board if a cycle is detected, treating it as a data integrity alarm rather than trying to prevent it at the edge. (6) Including dependency status in planning prompts is correct and important. The planning phase should inject a 'Dependency Context' section into the LLM prompt listing each depends_on entry with its current stage, title, and a one-line description. For resolved dependencies (done/rejected), include the resolution status. For in-progress dependencies, include a warning that the upstream design may change. This costs one list_all() call at planning time (already done for other purposes) and a few extra prompt tokens — negligible overhead for significantly better plan quality. The final consolidated implementation order should be: (1) state.py: add depends_on field with conditional persistence, resolve_dependencies() and detect_cycle() functions; (2) server.py: _cmd_edit_depends_on handler with validation, dependency_dirty cache flag, blocked status in broadcast; (3) server.py auto-run: skip blocked features in impl_stages only, with cycle-detection backstop; (4) server.py move_to_stage: rejection triggers plan_invalidation_check() writing to dependency_events, warning log on manual blocked-feature moves; (5) phases.py: inject dependency context into planning prompts, consume dependency_events during re-planning; (6) Go/WebUI: FeatureSummary additions, edit-depends-on endpoint; (7) TUI: dependency display and edit UI."
}
